var e={controls:{play:`▶ Play`,pause:`❚❚ Pause`,playAria:`Play`,pauseAria:`Pause`},kit:{previewCaption:`Example data, drawn like the board does. The buttons stand in for the push button, your phone and the serial monitor.`,galleryComing:`Coming with this lab.`,previewComing:`Preview coming with this lab.`},journey:{aria:`The eight Bluetooth labs as a path: what the board does in each and which way data flows.`,stageLabel:`{{id}} {{title}}: {{what}}`,stages:{B1:{title:`Listen`,role:`scanner`,what:`hears every device's advertising`,caption:`B1 Listen: the board is the scanner and hears every device's advertising. Data flows into the board.`},B2:{title:`Be seen`,role:`broadcaster`,what:`shouts its own live data`,caption:`B2 Be seen: the board is the broadcaster and shouts its own live data. Data flows out of the board.`},B3:{title:`Connect & talk`,role:`peripheral`,what:`is read and written by your phone`,caption:`B3 Connect & talk: the board is the peripheral and is read and written by your phone. Data flows both ways.`},B4:{title:`Pair & trust`,role:`peripheral + keys`,what:`adds encryption, passkeys and bonds`,caption:`B4 Pair & trust: the board is the peripheral + keys and adds encryption, passkeys and bonds. Data flows both ways.`},B5:{title:`Phone app`,role:`peripheral`,what:`talks to your own web app over Web Bluetooth`,caption:`B5 Phone app: the board is the peripheral and talks to your own web app over Web Bluetooth. Data flows both ways.`},B6:{title:`Keyboard & media`,role:`HID peripheral`,what:`types and changes the volume`,caption:`B6 Keyboard & media: the board is the HID peripheral and types and changes the volume. Data flows out of the board.`},B7:{title:`Music`,role:`MIDI peripheral`,what:`plays notes in GarageBand`,caption:`B7 Music: the board is the MIDI peripheral and plays notes in GarageBand. Data flows both ways.`},B8:{title:`Host`,role:`central`,what:`lets your keyboard type onto it`,caption:`B8 Host: the board is the central and lets your keyboard type onto it. Data flows into the board.`}}},radio:{window:`listening window`,windowAria:`Scan window in percent of the interval`,percent:`{{value, number}} %`,mode:`Scan mode`,passive:`passive scan`,active:`active scan`,aria:`The 2.4 GHz band with the 40 Bluetooth channels and three Wi-Fi channels, and below it a timeline of advertising packets on channels 37, 38 and 39 and the scanner's listening windows.`,caption:`The board listens {{value, number}} % of the time, one channel per 100 ms. Each device sends every packet three times, once per channel, so a listener on any one channel still hears it.`,activeNote:`Active: every packet heard from a scannable device gets a scan request back, and the device answers with a second packet (more names, more data).`,passiveNote:`Passive: the board never transmits.`,devices:{phone:`phone`,speaker:`speaker`,keyboard:`keyboard`},draw:{band:`2.4 GHz band: 40 Bluetooth LE channels, 2 MHz each`,listening:`listening`,channel:`ch {{ch}}`,tally:`advertising events heard {{heard, number}} · missed {{missed, number}} · {{caught, number}} % caught`,slowed:`time slowed down 20x`}},anatomy:{device:`Which device`,previous:`Previous field`,next:`Next field`,bytes:`Packet bytes`,used:`{{used, number}}/31 data bytes used`,position:`{{at, number}}/{{n, number}} · {{what}}`,samples:{phone:`an iPhone`,jbl:`a JBL speaker`,keyboard:`a BLE keyboard`,bench:`your B2 beacon`},pdu:{0:`ADV_IND (connectable, scannable)`,2:`ADV_NONCONN_IND (broadcast only)`,6:`ADV_SCAN_IND (scannable)`},field:{preamble:`preamble`,preambleWhat:`Preamble AAh: 10101010, so the receiver can lock onto the bit timing. Every advertising packet starts like this.`,access:`access address`,accessWhat:`Access address 8E89BED6h (sent little-endian): the fixed value for all advertising. A connection later gets its own random one.`,header:`header`,headerWhat:`PDU header: type {{pdu}} = {{pduName}}, TxAdd {{txAdd}} = {{kind}}, then the length: {{length}} bytes follow (6 address + {{data}} data).`,random:`random address`,public:`public address`,advaWhat:`The advertiser's address {{address}}, sent backwards (little-endian). {{kind}}`,addressResolvable:`Top bits 01: a resolvable private address that changes every ~15 min.`,addressRandom:`A random address.`,addressPublic:`A public address, burnt in at the factory.`,ad:`AD {{n}}`,adWhat:`AD structure: length {{length}}, type {{type}}. {{meaning}}`,crcWhat:`CRC-24 over header, address and data: the receiver recomputes it and throws the packet away if one bit flipped. (On air every bit after the access address is also “whitened”, scrambled with a pattern that depends on the channel, to avoid long runs of zeros.)`},ad:{discoverable:{general:`general discoverable`,limited:`limited discoverable (only for a while, like pairing mode)`,none:`not discoverable`},flagsLeOnly:`Flags {{value}}: {{mode}}, LE only (no Classic Bluetooth).`,flagsClassic:`Flags {{value}}: {{mode}}, Classic Bluetooth too.`,name:`Complete name: “{{name}}”. Plain ASCII, no terminating zero: the length byte says where it ends.`,tx:`TX power {{dbm}} dBm: how loud it shouts, so a listener can estimate distance.`,appearance:`Appearance {{value}}h (little-endian {{bytes}}): 03C1 = keyboard. The category is the top 10 bits.`,services:`Complete list of 16-bit services: {{value}}h = HID, the keyboard/mouse service. A host knows from the advert that this can type.`,apple:`Manufacturer data, company 004Ch = Apple (written {{bytes}}: little-endian). Then type {{type}} = Nearby info, {{count}} bytes of status (screen on, Wi-Fi on, an encrypted hint). This is how iPhones find each other.`,jbl:`Manufacturer data, company 0057h = Harman (JBL). The rest is JBL's own format for its app: model, colour, battery, PartyBoost state. Nobody but JBL documents it.`,bench:`Manufacturer data, company FFFFh = the test ID, used by our B2 beacon: version 01, presses {{presses, number}}, temperature {{celsius, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} °C, uptime {{uptime, number}} s. All little-endian.`,maker:`Manufacturer data.`}},timing:{sliders:{adv:{label:`advert every`,aria:`Advertising interval in ms`},si:{label:`scan interval`,aria:`Scan interval in ms`},win:{label:`window`,aria:`Scan window in percent of the scan interval`}},ms:`{{value, number}} ms`,delay:`random 0-10 ms advDelay`,aria:`Timeline: the board's advertising events on channels 37, 38 and 39, the scanner's listening windows, caught packets in green and missed ones in grey.`,count:`{{caught, number}} of {{total, number}}`,rest:`advertising events heard ({{pct, number}} %). The scanner listens {{listen, number}} % of the time, one channel per scan interval.`,restFirst:`advertising events heard ({{pct, number}} %). The scanner listens {{listen, number}} % of the time, one channel per scan interval; first heard after {{first, number}} ms.`,stuck:`No random delay and intervals that divide evenly:`,stuckWhy:`the two clocks stay in lockstep, so the same packets get missed forever. That's what advDelay prevents.`,longer:`Longer interval = fewer chances = the phone notices the board later, but the battery lasts longer.`,draw:{now:`now`,span:`{{ms, number}} ms shown, at a quarter of real speed`,listening:`scanner listening`,caught:`caught`,missed:`missed (nobody listening on that channel)`}},builder:{preset:`mode {{name}}`,presetB3:`B3's advert`,field:`Field`,bytes:`Bytes`,goesIn:`Goes in`,where:`Where {{name}} goes`,place:{none:`left out`,adv:`advertisement`,rsp:`scan response`},name:`Name`,nameAria:`Device name`,url:`URL`,urlAria:`Eddystone URL`,aria:`Two rows of 31 byte cells, the advertisement and the scan response, coloured per field. Cells past 31 are red: they don't fit.`,row:`{{label}}: {{count, number}} of 31 bytes`,rowOver:`{{label}}: {{count, number}} of 31 bytes · {{over, number}} too many`,empty:`empty: no scan response is sent`,detail:`<b>{{name}}</b> ({{length, number}} + 2 bytes): {{why}}`,badUrl:`<b>This URL doesn't encode:</b> it must start with http(s):// and be at most 17 bytes after that.`,fields:{flags:{name:`Flags`,why:`02 01 06: LE General Discoverable, Classic not supported. Phones skip adverts that aren't discoverable.`},name:{name:`Name`,why:`Complete Local Name: what people read in the list. Each letter is one byte.`},tx:{name:`TX power`,why:`The power the board sends with (+9 dBm). A phone compares it with the RSSI it measures to guess distance.`},app:{name:`Appearance`,why:`0540 = generic sensor. Phones pick an icon from it (0x03C1 would be a keyboard).`},svc16:{name:`16-bit service`,why:`180F, the Battery service, little-endian: standard services fit in 2 bytes.`},svc128:{name:`128-bit service`,why:`The Bench service UUID: our own services need the full 16 bytes, more than half the budget. B3 puts it here so apps can filter by it.`},maker:{name:`Maker FFFF`,why:`Manufacturer data: 2 bytes company ID (FFFF = test), then anything you like: B2's version, presses, temperature, uptime.`},ibeacon:{name:`iBeacon`,why:`Apple's manufacturer data: 4C 00 02 15, UUID, major, minor, power at 1 m. 27 bytes: nothing else but Flags fits.`},url:{name:`Eddystone-URL`,why:`Service data for FEAA: frame 10, power at 0 m, scheme byte, then the URL with .com/ and friends as single bytes.`}}},legend:{b3:`radio packet or call (orange: this step)`,b4:`SMP or link-layer packet (orange: this step)`,b4Wait:`waiting for a person`,b5:`call, event or radio packet (orange: this step)`,b8:`packet on the air (orange: this step)`,b8Wait:`the board waiting for you`},table:{aria:`The phone on the left, the board's GATT table on the right; a packet travels between them for the chosen operation.`,phone:`phone`,service:`Bench service ba5e0000-… (board)`,operations:`Operations`,toPhone:`board → phone`,toBoard:`phone → board`,what:`: {{what}}.`,value:`Value: {{value}}`,ops:{read:{action:`read {{name}}`,what:`Read Request (0A) + handle → Read Response (0B) + value`},write:{action:`write {{name}}`,what:`Write Request (12) + handle + value → Write Response (13)`},subscribe:{action:`subscribe {{name}}`,what:`a Write Request into the CCCD descriptor (handle + 1): 01 00 = notifications on`},notify:{action:`notify {{name}}`,what:`Handle Value Notification (1B) + handle + value, no answer`}},show:{cccd:`CCCD = notifications on`,gesture:`gesture {{gesture}} ({{name}}), count {{count, number}}`,celsius:`{{value, number}} °C`,seconds:`{{value, number}} s`}},matrix:{phone:`phone (starts pairing)`,phoneAria:`Phone's IO capability`,board:`board`,boardAria:`Board's IO capability`,mitm:`MITM protection asked`,aria:`The phone and the board: who shows the number and who types or confirms it, for the chosen capabilities.`,initiator:`phone (initiator)`,responder:`board (responder)`,unchecked:`encrypted, but nobody checked: no MITM protection`,checked:`a person checks: protected against a man in the middle`,methodTitle:`{{method}}.`,corner:`phone ↓ · board →`,sixDigits:`⌨ 6 digits`,methods:{J:`Just Works`,N:`Numeric Comparison`,PI:`Passkey Entry`,PR:`Passkey Entry`,PB:`Passkey Entry (both type)`},short:{J:`Just Works`,N:`Numeric Comparison`,PI:`Passkey · board shows`,PR:`Passkey · phone shows`,PB:`Passkey (both)`},roles:{nothing:`nothing to do`,tapYes:`same? tap yes`,pressYes:`same? press yes`,types:`types it`,shows:`shows it`,typeSame:`you type the same`,onBoth:`on both`},why:{JNoMitm:`Neither side asked for MITM protection, so the stack takes the easy way, whatever the screens can do.`,JNoInput:`One side can neither show nor enter anything (or both can only show), so there is nothing a person could compare. The link is encrypted against eavesdroppers, but an attacker relaying both sides could have done the same. B4's Vault refuses this: it demands authentication.`,N:`Both sides have a screen and can say yes: each computes six digits from the exchanged public keys. A man in the middle would have different keys, so the numbers would differ. Only with LE Secure Connections.`,PI:`The board shows six random digits; you type them on the phone. Both sides prove, bit by bit, that they know the same passkey before they agree on keys.`,PR:`The phone shows the digits and the board types them. B4's board can't type, so you'll never see this one in the lab: it's how a keyboard pairs (B8).`,PB:`Neither side has a screen: you invent six digits and type them on both keyboards.`}},throughput:{sliders:{mtu:{label:`MTU`,aria:`MTU in bytes`},interval:{label:`Connection interval`,aria:`Connection interval in ms`},cap:{label:`Radio packets the central allows per event`,aria:`Packets per connection event`}},mtuValue:`{{mtu, number}} B ({{data, number}} B of data)`,ms:`{{value, number}} ms`,count:`{{value, number}}`,longOn:`long packets: on (251 B)`,longOff:`long packets: off (27 B)`,aria:`Three connection events on a time line, with every radio packet drawn to scale.`,event:`event {{n, number}} · {{ms, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} ms`,data:`board → central: notification packets`,acks:`central → board: empty acknowledgements (below the line)`,shown:`{{ms, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} ms shown · the radios sleep in the gaps`,result_one:`<b>{{kBps, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} kB/s</b> = {{count, number}} notification × {{data, number}} B per event ÷ {{interval, number}} ms.`,result_other:`<b>{{kBps, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} kB/s</b> = {{count, number}} notifications × {{data, number}} B per event ÷ {{interval, number}} ms.`,frags_one:`Each notification is {{count, number}} radio packet ({{ms, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} ms with acknowledgements).`,frags_other:`Each notification is {{count, number}} radio packets ({{ms, number(minimumFractionDigits: 2; maximumFractionDigits: 2)}} ms with acknowledgements).`,limit:{cap:`Limited by the central's packets per event.`,time:`Limited by air time: the event is full.`},nothing:`<b>Nothing fits:</b> raise the packets per event or shorten the MTU.`},sound:{keys:`Media keys`,key:{play:`play/pause`,next:`next track`,down:`volume −`,up:`volume +`},directButton:`board → speaker directly?`,aria:`The board sends a media key to the phone over Bluetooth LE; the phone streams audio to the speaker over Classic Bluetooth`,start:`Press a media key.`,sent:`The board sends one tiny HID report (usage {{usage}}) and a release. The phone does the rest: it changes its own music, and its audio stream to the speaker follows.`,direct:`The S3's radio speaks only Bluetooth LE. Speaker audio uses Classic Bluetooth (BR/EDR), another modulation and protocol, so the board can't even see the JBL's audio side. The original ESP32 has Classic and can stream (A2DP); newer speakers with LE Audio/Auracast need Bluetooth 5.2, the S3 has 5.0.`,draw:{leOnly:`LE only`,phone:`phone`,orMac:`or Mac`,playing:`▶ playing`,paused:`❚❚ paused`,volume:`vol {{value, number}}`,speaker:`JBL speaker`,classic:`Classic BT audio`,reports:`2-8 byte reports`,rate:`~ 300 kbit/s, nonstop`,fail:`✕ different radio: LE packets mean nothing to an A2DP sink`}},report:{layouts:`Layouts`,boardLayout:`board's <code>layout</code>`,boardAria:`Board layout`,computer:`computer set to`,computerAria:`Computer layout`,us:`US`,de:`German`,text:`Text to type`,type:`type`,clear:`clear`,aria:`A key press travelling from the board's character table, as an 8-byte report, to the computer's layout table`,mismatchUs:`The board looks characters up in its {{board}} table but the computer reads key numbers with US: y and z swap, punctuation lands elsewhere. Fix: layout {{host}}.`,mismatchDe:`The board looks characters up in its {{board}} table but the computer reads key numbers with German: y and z swap, punctuation lands elsewhere. Fix: layout {{host}}.`,agree:`Both sides agree, so every character survives the trip.`,agreeSkipped:`Both sides agree, so every character survives the trip. Skipped (needs AltGr/Option): {{skipped}}`,draw:{board:`the board`,report:`8-byte report`,overLe:`over Bluetooth LE`,computer:`the computer`,setUs:`set to US`,setDe:`set to German`,noShift:`mod 00 = no Shift`,typed:`you typed`,shows:`the computer shows`}},packet:{options:`Packet options`,running:`running status`,collect:`collect for`,interval:`Connection interval`,ms:`{{value, number}} ms`,slow:`300 ms (slow, to see batching)`,hear:`hear it`,keyboard:`Mini keyboard`,keyAria:`{{name}} (key {{key}})`,notes:{C:`C`,D:`D`,E:`E`,F:`F`,G:`G`,A:`A`,B:`B`},aria:`The bytes of the last BLE-MIDI packet, coloured by role`,start:`Play a note.`,built_one:`{{count, number}} note in one packet, {{real, number}} bytes.`,built_other:`{{count, number}} notes in one packet, {{real, number}} bytes.`,builtSaved_one:`{{count, number}} note in one packet, {{real, number}} bytes: running status saved {{saved, number}}.`,builtSaved_other:`{{count, number}} notes in one packet, {{real, number}} bytes: running status saved {{saved, number}}.`,timestamps:`Each note keeps its own timestamp, so the synth can space them as you played them. A note off goes out 300 ms later the same way (status 80).`,empty:`Play a few notes: they gather into one packet.`,legend:{header:`header: top bit 1, then the clock's high bits`,ts:`timestamp: top bit 1, low 7 bits of the ms clock`,status:`status: what + channel (90 = note on, ch 1)`,note:`note number (60 = middle C)`,vel:`velocity (how hard)`}},latency:{group:`Interval and rhythm`,play:`play`,rhythm:`Rhythm`,rhythms:{sixteenths:`sixteenths at 240 bpm (62.5 ms)`,trill:`a fast trill (40 ms)`,eighths:`eighths at 240 bpm (125 ms)`},aria:`Notes played, the connection events that carry them, and when two kinds of synth play them`,lanes:{play:`you play`,events:`radio events`,arrival:`synth: on arrival`,timestamps:`synth: timestamps`},caption:`Interval {{interval, number}} ms: each note waits 0 to {{interval, number}} ms for the next radio event. A synth that plays notes on arrival moves the gaps between them by up to {{err, number(minimumFractionDigits: 1; maximumFractionDigits: 1)}} ms (red). With the timestamps it keeps your timing exactly and plays everything {{interval, number}} ms late (green).`},decoder:{keyboardScreen:`The board's keyboard view: {{text}}▌`,empty:`(empty)`,mouseScreen:`The board's mouse view: cursor at {{x}}, {{y}}, brush {{brush}}.`,pressKey:`Press a key: one report on the way down, one on the way up.`,drag:`Drag on the pad: every movement is one report.`,backspace:`backspace`,enter:`enter`,char:`“{{c}}”`,keyUs:`key {{code}} with mods {{mods}} → {{typed}} on a US layout`,keyDe:`key {{code}} with mods {{mods}} → {{typed}} on a German layout`,releaseKey:`release: every key slot is 00 again, nothing typed`,media:`media key {{usage}}: {{name}}`,mediaName:{up:`volume up`,down:`volume down`,play:`play/pause`},releaseMedia:`release: 00 00`,mouse:`buttons {{buttons}} · X {{dx}} · Y {{dy}} · wheel {{wheel}}`,group:`Report Map and layout`,map:`Report Map`,maps:{combo:`Keyboard + media keys`,boot:`Boot keyboard (HID spec)`,mouse:`5-button mouse, 12-bit X/Y`},layout:`Layout`,layoutAria:`Keyboard layout`,german:`German layout: the key with code 1C now types z, and 2D types ß. The keyboard sends the same codes.`,us:`US layout.`,usOption:`US (QWERTY)`,deOption:`German (QWERTZ)`,aria:`The report travelling from the device to the board, its bytes and bits, each field lit in turn`,keyboard:`Keyboard`,keyAria:`key code {{code}}: {{c}}`,nothing:`nothing`,space:`Space (2C)`,mediaKey:{down:`vol − (EA)`,up:`vol + (E9)`,play:`play (CD)`},field:{padding:`padding`,modifier:`modifier`,keys_one:`key codes ({{count, number}} slot)`,keys_other:`key codes ({{count, number}} slots)`,button:`button {{n}}`,x:`X (relative)`,y:`Y (relative)`,wheel:`wheel`,media:`media key (consumer usage)`,other:`other`},draw:{keyboard:`keyboard`,mouse:`mouse`,board:`board`,reportId:`report ID {{id}} (from 2908, not in the bytes)`,bits:`bits, bit 0 first in each byte (bright = 1)`,field_one:`{{name}}: {{count, number}} bit from bit {{bit}} = {{value}}`,field_other:`{{name}}: {{count, number}} bits from bit {{bit}} = {{value}}`}},pad:{aria:`Mouse pad: drag to move, press to click, scroll for the wheel, or use the arrow keys`,hint:`drag here = move · press = left button · scroll = wheel`,left:`left click`,right:`right click`,wheelUp:`wheel +1`,wheelDown:`wheel −1`},items:{title:`Report Map items`,size:`{{bytes, number}} bytes · {{fields, number}} input fields`},screens:{b1:{captions:{0:`Every device is a blip: the closer to the middle, the stronger its signal; the colour is its kind (blue phone, amber audio, teal keyboard, coral tag). On the right, the nearest six with a distance guess.`,1:`The strongest devices with their name or maker, address type (rpa = a phone hiding behind a private address), signal and how long ago it was heard. The picked one is highlighted.`,2:`The picked device's advertisement and scan response as 31 little cells, one per byte, coloured per AD structure, with each structure decoded underneath.`,3:`Packets per second over the last minute (bars) and devices in range (line), the scan mode and duty cycle, and the mix of address types.`},actions:{phoneCloser:`phone: closer`,phoneWalkAway:`phone: walk away`,doubleClickNext:`double-click: next`,longPressScan:`long press: scan`}},b2:{captions:{0:`The advertisement and the scan response as 31 cells each, one colour per AD structure (length and type bytes darker), decoded underneath. Bytes that just changed flash white: the uptime every second, the presses on a double-click.`,1:`The timing, drawn from the settings: one event per interval plus a random 0-10 ms (the +n labels), each event one packet on channels 37, 38 and 39. The rings leave the board when an event fires; the inset shows one event magnified (about 0.3 ms per packet).`,2:`An estimate for a coin-cell beacon chip with these settings: where the current goes, the average, the life on a CR2032, and life against interval (the amber dot = now). This board itself draws ~80 mA.`,3:`The activity log: mode changes, presses, refused settings.`},actions:{buttonDoubleClickPress:`button: double-click (press)`,modeNext:`mode: next`,connectableOnOff:`connectable on/off`,interval1001000:`interval 100 / 1000`}},b3:{captions:{0:`The Bench service as a table: name, properties (R read, W write, N notify) and the current value. An operation lights its row: teal read, blue write, violet notify, amber subscribe. A dot travels to or from the phone on the right; a bell means subscribed.`,1:`The last message written from the phone, word-wrapped in 12 pt, and the LED's colour as a glowing disc.`,2:`The live connection: who is central, the connection events ticking (slowed down 20x), interval, MTU, latency, timeout, PHY, RSSI, security, and the traffic counters.`,3:`The activity log, newest at the bottom: < received from the phone, > sent to it, + good news, ! warnings.`},actions:{phoneConnect:`phone: connect`,phoneWriteLedFf5000:`phone: write LED FF5000`,phoneWriteMessage:`phone: write Message`,phoneSubscribeButton:`phone: subscribe Button`,boardClick:`board: click`}},b4:{captions:{0:`The pairing ceremony as eight phases on a track: filled = reported by NimBLE, a ring = inferred (it must have happened for a later phase), amber pulse = waiting for you, coral cross = where it failed and why. Violet = encrypted and bonded.`,1:`The bonded devices by identity address, and the privacy trick: the phone's address on air keeps changing; the IRK from bonding resolves every one of them to the same phone.`,2:`B3's gatt view plus the Vault row. A refused Vault read flashes coral.`,3:`The live connection; the security line now says encrypted, + MITM, + bonded.`,4:`The activity log: passkeys, ENC_CHANGE results, identities, refusals.`},actions:{phoneConnect:`phone: connect`,phoneReadVault:`phone: read Vault`,phoneTypeThePasskeyTapYes:`phone: type the passkey / tap yes`,boardClickYes:`board: click (yes)`}},b5:{captions:{0:`What the app draws, copied under the status bar every frame (the board keeps it in its own 320 × 128 buffer), and at the bottom how many commands, writes and bytes have arrived. DROPPED means the queue of 16 writes was full.`,1:`The four values the app charts, as sparklines over the last 12 s: temperature, touch on GPIO 1, free heap, link RSSI. Bottom right: whether the app subscribed, and how many notifications went out.`,2:`While a run goes: packets flying to the phone, a progress bar and the waits (the stack's buffers were full). Afterwards one bar per run with its kB/s, MTU, interval and PHY.`,3:`B3's Bench service, unchanged: the rows light when the app reads, writes or subscribes. B5's own four characteristics don't appear here; the other views show them.`,4:`The live connection: interval, MTU, PHY and RSSI. Watch them change when the Speed tab's Link buttons ask for a faster interval or the 2M PHY.`,5:`The activity log: < from the app, > to the app, + results such as each speed run.`},actions:{appConnect:`app: connect`,appDrawAStroke:`app: draw a stroke`,appSubscribeSensors:`app: subscribe Sensors`,touchTheWireOnGpio1:`touch the wire on GPIO 1`,appSpeed50Kb:`app: speed 50 kB`,appFast2m:`app: fast + 2M`}},b6:{captions:{0:`Mode: the button is the remote. The icon you pressed ripples; the dot runs board → phone → speaker.`,1:`Mode: a slide deck. Click = right arrow, double = left arrow, hold = b (black screen).`,2:"What `type` is sending: the layout, progress, the snippet, and the computer's Caps/Num lights sent back to us.",3:`The last reports as bytes: modifier bits, key numbers, what each press types, the all-zero release.`,4:`The connection: interval, MTU, security (a keyboard link is always encrypted).`,5:`Everything that happened on the air.`},actions:{phonePairBenchB6:`phone: pair Bench B6`,buttonClick:`button: click`,typeYes:`type yes`}},b7:{captions:{0:`Five touch keys and a two-octave piano: violet = notes this board plays, blue = notes from the synth.`,1:`The drum machine: 4 drums x 16 steps on channel 10, the playhead walking at the tempo.`,2:`A piano roll of the last 6 seconds, both directions, with how many notes shared a packet.`,3:`Why notes wait: each one leaves at the next connection event.`,4:`Everything that happened on the air.`},actions:{macConnectInAudioMidiSetup:`Mac: connect in Audio MIDI Setup`,buttonClickANote:`button: click (a note)`,synthPlayE4:`synth: play E4`,seqOnOff:`seq on/off`}},b8:{captions:{0:`Connectable devices only, strongest first. A keyboard icon and HID mark keyboards and mice; a key marks devices bonded to the board. The highlighted row is the picked one.`,1:`The connected device's GATT table: services (blue), characteristics with R/W/N/I and the public values that were read (teal), descriptors (grey). HID values stay locked until paired.`,2:`Your keyboard, typing onto the board: the text with a blinking cursor, the eight modifier caps (amber while held), the held key codes, layout and protocol.`,3:`Your mouse as a paint toy: left button draws, right changes colour, middle clears, the wheel sets the brush. The trail shows where the cursor just was.`,4:`The raw input reports, newest at the bottom: each byte coloured by its Report Map field (amber modifiers/buttons, teal keys, blue X, violet Y, coral wheel), decoded underneath.`,5:`The live link from the central's side: the board leads, the keyboard answers in each connection event. Interval, MTU, PHY, signal and security.`,6:`Everything that happened, in order: what the board sent (>), received (<), and the results.`},actions:{keyboardPairingModeConnectHost:`keyboard: pairing mode, connect, host`,typeHelloBench:`type “Hello bench”`,mediaVolumeUp:`media: volume up`,mouseConnectDraw:`mouse: connect + draw`,exploreTheJbl:`explore the JBL`,disconnect:`disconnect`}}},flows:JSON.parse(`{"b3":{"discover":{"label":"Connect & discover","lanes":[["phone","the central"],["NimBLE","board, peripheral"],["loop()","screen and LED"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"ADV_IND + SCAN_RSP","title":"The board advertises","text":"Flags and the 128-bit Bench service UUID in the advertisement, the name in the scan response. The phone's scanner shows Bench B3."},"1":{"msg":"CONNECT_IND","title":"The phone connects","text":"In answer to one advert, the phone sends the connection's settings: a new random access address, the hop pattern over 37 data channels, the interval and the timeout. From now on they meet every interval."},"2":{"msg":"onConnect","title":"The board takes note","text":"NimBLE calls ServerLink::onConnect in its own task. It only updates the shared link state and the activity log; loop() draws the two rings and prints the line."},"3":{"msg":"Exchange MTU 517","title":"How big may a packet be?","text":"By default an ATT packet carries 23 bytes. The phone offers more, the board answers what it can take (255); both use the smaller."},"4":{"msg":"Read By Group Type","title":"Which services are there?","text":"The phone asks for all primary services (UUID 2800) between handles 0001 and FFFF. The board answers with each service's first handle, last handle and UUID."},"5":{"msg":"Read By Type 2803","title":"…and which characteristics?","text":"For each service, the characteristic declarations: properties (a bit field), the value's handle and the UUID. 0A = read + write, 12 = read + notify."},"6":{"msg":"Find Information","title":"…and their descriptors","text":"Descriptors sit right after a value: 2902 (the CCCD, where you subscribe), 2901 (the name apps show), 2904 (number format: sint16, ×10^-2, °C)."},"7":{"msg":"Write CCCD 01 00","title":"Subscribe to the button","text":"Tapping the notify icon writes 0001 into the Button's CCCD. NimBLE calls onSubscribe: a bell appears on the row."}}},"write":{"label":"Write the LED","lanes":[["phone","nRF Connect"],["NimBLE","onWrite()"],["loop()","tick() → LED"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"Write Request","title":"Three bytes","text":"Opcode 12 (Write Request), handle 0010 (the LED value), then FF 50 00. It waits for the next connection event: up to one interval."},"1":{"msg":"onWrite","title":"The callback runs in NimBLE's task","text":"It checks there are exactly 3 bytes, copies them under the spinlock and raises a flag. It does not touch the LED: only one task ever drives it."},"2":{"msg":"Write Response","title":"The phone gets its OK","text":"Opcode 13. With “write without response” (opcode 52) there is no answer at all: faster, but the phone never learns whether it arrived."},"3":{"msg":"takeLed()","title":"loop() lights it","text":"The next loop() sees the flag, claims the LED from the status display and writes the colour to the WS2812 on GPIO 48. The gatt row flashes blue."}}},"notify":{"label":"Notify a click","lanes":[["phone","subscribed"],["NimBLE","the stack"],["loop()","button"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"click","title":"You click","text":"The Button class debounces it and reports a Click. The lab's gesture hook hands it to the Bench service, then the shell moves to the next view."},"1":{"msg":"notify()","title":"Set the value, notify","text":"Gesture 1, count 7 (little-endian). notify() queues it for the next connection event; nothing waits for an answer."},"2":{"msg":"Handle Value Notification","title":"On air","text":"Opcode 1B, the handle, the value. No response: notifications are fire and forget. (Indications, 1D, would wait for a 1E confirmation.)"}}}},"b4":{"passkey":{"label":"Passkey Entry","lanes":[["phone","central"],["board","security manager"],["you","a person"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"Read Vault","title":"The phone touches the Vault","text":"A plain ATT read of the Vault's handle. NimBLE checks the characteristic's permissions (READ_ENC + READ_AUTHEN) and refuses."},"1":{"msg":"Pairing Request","title":"Phase 1: what can you do?","text":"The phone starts pairing (SMP over its own L2CAP channel, CID 0006). It says it has a screen and a keyboard, wants to bond, wants MITM protection and Secure Connections."},"2":{"msg":"Pairing Response","title":"The board answers: DisplayOnly","text":"Its own capabilities, as set by the pair command. From both answers each side looks up the same method in the matrix above."},"3":{"msg":"Public Key","title":"Phase 2: elliptic-curve keys","text":"Each side sends a fresh P-256 public key (64 bytes: X and Y). Each multiplies the other's public key with its own private key: both get the same DHKey, which never travels."},"4":{"msg":"show 482915","title":"The board shows six digits","text":"onPassKeyDisplay returns a random passkey; the shell draws it over the current view."},"5":{"msg":"type 482915","title":"You type them on the phone","text":"The 30-second clock runs. Typing proves you see both screens: an attacker in the middle doesn't know the number."},"6":{"msg":"Confirm + Random ×20","title":"20 rounds, one per passkey bit","text":"The passkey is 20 bits. For each bit both sides commit to a value (Confirm), then reveal it (Random). A wrong digit fails on its bit, without giving the rest away."},"7":{"msg":"DHKey Check","title":"Phase 2 ends: prove the same DHKey","text":"Each side sends a check value computed (f6) from the DHKey, both randoms and both addresses. Only someone with the same DHKey can produce it."},"8":{"msg":"LL_ENC_REQ","title":"Switch on encryption","text":"The link layer starts AES-CCM with the LTK. From the next packet on, an eavesdropper sees noise. NimBLE reports ENC_CHANGE: the ceremony's encrypted phase."},"9":{"msg":"Identity Information","title":"Phase 3: keys to remember","text":"Now encrypted, both hand over their IRK and identity address. The IRK lets the board recognise the phone later, whatever private address it uses that day."},"10":{"msg":"bond saved","title":"Bonded, and authenticated","text":"Keys in NVS. The phone reads the Vault again and gets it. Next time: encrypted at once with the stored LTK, no ceremony."}}},"compare":{"label":"Numeric Comparison","lanes":[["phone","central"],["board","security manager"],["you","a person"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"Read Vault","title":"The phone touches the Vault","text":"A plain ATT read of the Vault's handle. NimBLE checks the characteristic's permissions (READ_ENC + READ_AUTHEN) and refuses."},"1":{"msg":"Pairing Request","title":"Phase 1: what can you do?","text":"The phone starts pairing (SMP over its own L2CAP channel, CID 0006). It says it has a screen and a keyboard, wants to bond, wants MITM protection and Secure Connections."},"2":{"msg":"Pairing Response","title":"The board answers: DisplayYesNo","text":"Its own capabilities, as set by the pair command. From both answers each side looks up the same method in the matrix above."},"3":{"msg":"Public Key","title":"Phase 2: elliptic-curve keys","text":"Each side sends a fresh P-256 public key (64 bytes: X and Y). Each multiplies the other's public key with its own private key: both get the same DHKey, which never travels."},"4":{"msg":"Confirm","title":"The board commits","text":"The board sends a commitment to its random number first, so it can't change it after seeing the phone's."},"5":{"msg":"Random ×2","title":"Both reveal their randoms","text":"Now both compute g2(PKa, PKb, Na, Nb) mod 1,000,000: six digits that depend on both public keys."},"6":{"msg":"same 731406?","title":"Both screens show the number","text":"onConfirmPassKey: the TFT shows NUMERIC COMPARISON. You check the phone shows the same, tap yes there and click the board's button (long press = no)."},"7":{"msg":"DHKey Check","title":"Phase 2 ends: prove the same DHKey","text":"Each side sends a check value computed (f6) from the DHKey, both randoms and both addresses. Only someone with the same DHKey can produce it."},"8":{"msg":"LL_ENC_REQ","title":"Switch on encryption","text":"The link layer starts AES-CCM with the LTK. From the next packet on, an eavesdropper sees noise. NimBLE reports ENC_CHANGE: the ceremony's encrypted phase."},"9":{"msg":"Identity Information","title":"Phase 3: keys to remember","text":"Now encrypted, both hand over their IRK and identity address. The IRK lets the board recognise the phone later, whatever private address it uses that day."},"10":{"msg":"bond saved","title":"Bonded, and authenticated","text":"Keys in NVS. The phone reads the Vault again and gets it. Next time: encrypted at once with the stored LTK, no ceremony."}}},"justworks":{"label":"Just Works","lanes":[["phone","central"],["board","security manager"],["you","a person"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"Read Vault","title":"The phone touches the Vault","text":"A plain ATT read of the Vault's handle. NimBLE checks the characteristic's permissions (READ_ENC + READ_AUTHEN) and refuses."},"1":{"msg":"Pairing Request","title":"Phase 1: what can you do?","text":"The phone starts pairing (SMP over its own L2CAP channel, CID 0006). It says it has a screen and a keyboard, wants to bond, wants MITM protection and Secure Connections."},"2":{"msg":"Pairing Response","title":"The board answers: NoInputNoOutput","text":"Its own capabilities, as set by the pair command. From both answers each side looks up the same method in the matrix above."},"3":{"msg":"Public Key","title":"Phase 2: elliptic-curve keys","text":"Each side sends a fresh P-256 public key (64 bytes: X and Y). Each multiplies the other's public key with its own private key: both get the same DHKey, which never travels."},"4":{"msg":"Confirm + Random","title":"No person involved","text":"The same exchange as numeric comparison, but no screen shows the number: nobody can notice a man in the middle."},"5":{"msg":"DHKey Check","title":"Phase 2 ends: prove the same DHKey","text":"Each side sends a check value computed (f6) from the DHKey, both randoms and both addresses. Only someone with the same DHKey can produce it."},"6":{"msg":"LL_ENC_REQ","title":"Switch on encryption","text":"The link layer starts AES-CCM with the LTK. From the next packet on, an eavesdropper sees noise. NimBLE reports ENC_CHANGE: the ceremony's encrypted phase."},"7":{"msg":"Identity Information","title":"Phase 3: keys to remember","text":"Now encrypted, both hand over their IRK and identity address. The IRK lets the board recognise the phone later, whatever private address it uses that day."},"8":{"msg":"bond saved","title":"Bonded, but not authenticated","text":"Keys in NVS, link encrypted, but nobody verified it: the security level is “encrypted, no MITM”. The Vault still refuses; LED and Message work even with lock on."}}}},"b5":{"write":{"label":"Colour change","lanes":[["app (JS)","ble-app"],["browser","Chrome's GATT client"],["NimBLE","board, onWrite()"],["loop()","LED on GPIO 48"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"requestDevice()","title":"Ask the person to pick a board","text":"A page can't scan or connect by itself. requestDevice() opens the browser's chooser, filtered to boards with the Bench service, a name starting with “Bench”, or BLE-MIDI. Only the services listed here (plus optionalServices) will ever be reachable."},"1":{"msg":"CONNECT_IND","title":"The browser connects","text":"device.gatt.connect(): the operating system's Bluetooth stack becomes the central. The app never sees radio packets, only GATT objects."},"2":{"msg":"discover services","title":"Discovery (the same as nRF Connect)","text":"getPrimaryServices() and getCharacteristics() run the ATT discovery from B3 under the hood. The app keeps a handle per characteristic it knows by UUID."},"3":{"msg":"writeValueWithResponse()","title":"The wheel moved","text":"The Control tab turns hue, saturation and brightness into red, green, blue, at most once per 80 ms, and queues one write. GATT allows one operation at a time, so every call waits its turn in the app's queue."},"4":{"msg":"Write Request","title":"On air","text":"Opcode 12, handle 0010, three bytes. It leaves at the next connection event: up to one interval later."},"5":{"msg":"onWrite","title":"NimBLE's task takes it","text":"BenchService::onWrite checks the length, copies the colour under the spinlock and raises a flag. It never drives the LED itself: only loop() does."},"6":{"msg":"Write Response","title":"The promise resolves","text":"Opcode 13 comes back in the next event; the browser resolves the app's await. A refused write would reject it with the ATT error instead."},"7":{"msg":"takeLed()","title":"loop() lights the LED","text":"The next frame sees the flag, claims the LED from the radio status and writes the WS2812. The gatt row flashes blue."}}},"notify":{"label":"Sensor stream","lanes":[["app (JS)","Live tab"],["browser","GATT client"],["NimBLE","board"],["loop()","tick() every 200 ms"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"startNotifications()","title":"Subscribe","text":"The Live tab calls startNotifications() on Sensors. The browser writes 01 00 into its CCCD for us."},"1":{"msg":"Write CCCD 01 00","title":"The CCCD write on air","text":"Opcode 12 to the descriptor right after the value (handle + 1). NimBLE calls Extras::onSubscribe: from now on tick() sends."},"2":{"msg":"notify()","title":"Every 200 ms","text":"tick() reads the chip's temperature, touchRead(1), the free heap and the link's RSSI, packs 12 bytes and notifies."},"3":{"msg":"Handle Value Notification","title":"On air, no answer","text":"Opcode 1B, the handle, 12 bytes. Notifications are never acknowledged at the GATT level: five a second cost the phone almost nothing."},"4":{"msg":"characteristicvaluechanged","title":"An event in the page","text":"The browser fires an event with the value as a DataView. decodeSensors() reads it little-endian; the sequence number shows any gap."},"5":{"msg":"chart","title":"React draws the line","text":"The sample joins the last 150 (30 s) and the four charts redraw. Touch the wire: the next packet already carries the jump."}}}},"b8":{"first":{"label":"First pairing","lanes":[["ESP32-S3","central (this board)"],["keyboard","peripheral"],["you","at the keyboard"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"ADV_IND (ch 37, 38, 39)","title":"The keyboard advertises","text":"In pairing mode the keyboard shouts on the three advertising channels every 20-30 ms. Its HID service (1812) and its appearance (03C1 = keyboard) say what it is: the board's scanner marks it HID."},"1":{"msg":"CONNECT_IND","title":"The board connects: now IT sets the timing","text":"150 µs after an advertisement the board answers on the same channel. From here on both radios hop together over 37 data channels. As the central, the board chose the interval, the latency and the timeout."},"2":{"msg":"Exchange MTU: 255","title":"How big may a packet be?","text":"The default ATT packet is 23 bytes. The board offers 255; the keyboard answers with what it can do."},"3":{"msg":"MTU 65","title":"They agree on the smaller one","text":"65 bytes: 62 of data per packet. Plenty: a key press is 8 bytes."},"4":{"msg":"Read By Group Type (services)","title":"Discovery: what do you offer?","text":"The board asks for every primary service, then for the characteristics of each and their descriptors. No encryption needed yet: the table of contents is public."},"5":{"msg":"1800 1801 180A 180F 1812","title":"Five services","text":"Generic Access and Attribute (every device), Device Information, Battery, and HID with its Report Map and Report characteristics. The explore view shows this tree. The HID values are locked: they need an encrypted link."},"6":{"msg":"Pairing Request","title":"host: ask for security","text":"The board says what it can do: show digits, but not type them. It wants a bond (remember the keys), MITM protection and LE Secure Connections."},"7":{"msg":"Pairing Response","title":"The keyboard can only type","text":"Display only (board) + keyboard only (keyboard) = passkey entry: the side with the screen shows six digits, the side with the keys types them. Both exchange public keys (ECDH P-256) next."},"8":{"msg":"shows 482915","title":"Six digits on the TFT","text":"The passkey itself never goes over the air. The board draws it big over whatever view is showing and waits up to 30 s."},"9":{"msg":"types 4 8 2 9 1 5 ⏎","title":"You type them on the keyboard","text":"The keyboard is not connected to any computer that would show what you type: its key presses go into the pairing, not to an app."},"10":{"msg":"20 confirm rounds + DHKey check","title":"Proof, one bit at a time","text":"For each of the 20 bits of the passkey both sides exchange a commitment and then the random value behind it. Someone listening learns nothing they could use; someone in the middle would need the passkey."},"11":{"msg":"LL_ENC_REQ","title":"Encryption on, keys saved","text":"From now on every packet is encrypted (AES-CCM). The long-term key (LTK) and the keyboard's identity key (IRK) are stored in the board's NVS: that is the bond."},"12":{"msg":"Read Blob 2A4B","title":"Read the Report Map","text":"The keyboard's own description of its reports. It's longer than one packet, so it arrives in a few Read Blob rounds."},"13":{"msg":"Report Map, 72 bytes","title":"What every bit means","text":"Report 1: 8 modifier bits, a padding byte, 6 key codes. Report 3: one 16-bit media key. kit/lib/ble-kit/src/hid_report.h turns these bytes into fields; try it in the next panel."},"14":{"msg":"Read 2908 → 01 01","title":"Which Report is which?","text":"Each Report characteristic has a Report Reference descriptor: report ID and type (1 input, 2 output, 3 feature). The report ID never travels inside the notifications: this is the only place it lives."},"15":{"msg":"Write CCCD 01 00","title":"Subscribe","text":"Writing 01 00 into each input report's CCCD (descriptor 2902) means: send me notifications. Done for every input report."},"16":{"msg":"presses h","title":"You press h","text":"The keyboard scans its key matrix, finds the key at position 0B, and queues a report for the next connection event."},"17":{"msg":"Notify 00 00 0B 00 00 00 00 00","title":"One key press, 8 bytes","text":"No modifiers (00), a reserved byte (00), and the key code 0B in the first key slot. The board compares with the previous report: 0B is new, so it's a key press. 0B is h on US and German keyboards alike."},"18":{"msg":"Notify 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00","title":"Release","text":"When you let go, another report: all key slots empty. Holding a key sends nothing more: the board knows it is still down from the last report."}}},"back":{"label":"Coming back (bonded)","lanes":[["ESP32-S3","central (this board)"],["keyboard","peripheral"],["you","at the keyboard"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"presses any key","title":"Wake it up","text":"After a power cycle the board scans again. The keyboard sleeps until you press a key."},"1":{"msg":"ADV_IND / ADV_DIRECT_IND","title":"The keyboard calls its host","text":"A bonded keyboard often uses directed advertising: a packet addressed to the board only. The board's scan callback compares the address with its bonds in NVS."},"2":{"msg":"CONNECT_IND","title":"Connect","text":"The same as the first time: the board sets the timing."},"3":{"msg":"LL_ENC_REQ (stored LTK)","title":"Encrypt with the saved key","text":"No pairing, no passkey: both sides still have the long-term key from the bond. If one side forgot it, this fails with “PIN or key missing”: forget and pair again."},"4":{"msg":"Report Map + CCCD 01 00","title":"Host again","text":"The board reads the Report Map again and subscribes. (A bonded device remembers subscriptions, but the board doesn't rely on it.)"},"5":{"msg":"Notify …","title":"Type on","text":"The first key you pressed to wake it is usually lost; the next ones arrive."}}},"jbl":{"label":"Exploring your JBL","lanes":[["ESP32-S3","central (this board)"],["JBL speaker","BLE side"],["phone","plays the music"]],"steps":{"0":{"msg":"audio (Classic Bluetooth, A2DP)","title":"The music never touches BLE","text":"Your phone streams audio to the speaker over Classic Bluetooth (BR/EDR, the A2DP profile). The ESP32-S3 only has the Low Energy radio: it cannot join this."},"1":{"msg":"ADV_IND","title":"But the speaker also speaks BLE","text":"Many speakers advertise over BLE too, for their phone app (settings, firmware updates, linking speakers)."},"2":{"msg":"CONNECT_IND + discovery","title":"Explore","text":"connect <n>: the board connects and lists what it offers."},"3":{"msg":"Read 2A00 (device name)","title":"Only the public basics","text":"The board reads the standard values a speaker exposes to anyone, and nothing in the vendor service: that is the JBL app's own protocol."},"4":{"msg":"verdict","title":"Why there is no audio service","text":"No 1812, no standard audio service: just the app's own service. The board says so on the explore view and leaves it alone."}}}}}`)};export{e as default};
